{"parentUuid":"3803e965-557f-40c0-9014-b43696b81827","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a36348b9735cf680f","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZFQ1N64ks7pwoXLAQvJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01P7sKMKrSRSJf4XSrTo5FPY","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-skills/64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82/scratchpad/f.txt"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":813,"cache_read_input_tokens":49347,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":813,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01P7sKMKrSRSJf4XSrTo5FPY":{"file_path":"/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-skills/64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82/scratchpad/f.txt"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfZFPzu4Y6YobdWpTGQCN","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"10f72c79-0142-4235-86eb-9eee91e98622","timestamp":"2026-09-30T07:43:14.592Z","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1","slug":"warm-pondering-kernighan"}
{"parentUuid":"10f72c79-0142-4235-86eb-9eee91e98622","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"8c0f41b5-446a-49e2-8833-723d238c3f62","agentId":"a36348b9735cf680f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P7sKMKrSRSJf4XSrTo5FPY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t===== K-ARCH-3 ARCH.ORG.escalation; docs/00 §2; skills.json workstreams rendering, simulation, content, quality, online, foundation\n2\tF: Six workstreams contain more than one lead or cross-cutting subtree: render+gpu-platform, physics+animation, content-pipeline+resource-streaming, test+security, network+security (anti-cheat), core+4 cross-cutting. A cross-lead dispute inside one workstream is neither inside one subtree nor cross-workstream, so no arbiter is named. These are the highest-friction seams: ragdoll/root motion; ray-tracing-infrastructure under gpu-platform binding render-architect's C-RSCENE/C-INSTANCES; cook vs runtime format; anti-cheat validators vs replication.\n3\tP: Add an 'arbiter' per workstream in skills.json (e.g. rendering→render-architect, simulation→physics-architect with animation-architect as co-signer on sim-schedule, content→resource-streaming-architect for runtime and content-pipeline-architect for cook), or state explicitly in ARCH.ORG.escalation that same-workstream cross-lead disputes go to engine-architect. Have check.py require an arbiter for every multi-lead workstream.\n4\t V fact True I checked the finding against data/skills.json and it holds. Every lead and cross-cutting skill is a direct child of engine-architect, so one lead's subtree never contains another lead. Six workstreams contain more than one subtree: - rendering: render-architect (15 skills) and gpu-platform-architect (11). ray-tracing-infrastructure sits under gpu-platform-architect and consumes C-RSCENE and C-INSTANCES. - simulation: physics-architect and animation-architect. - content: content-pipeline-architect and resource-streaming-architect. - quality: test-architect and security-engineering. - online: network-architect, plus anti-cheat-integrity, which sits under security-engineering. - foundation: co\n5\t V materiality True The facts check out. In skills.json, render-architect and gpu-platform-architect are both children of engine-architect and both sit in the rendering workstream. physics-architect and animation-architect are both in simulation. content-pipeline-architect and resource-streaming-architect are both in content. test-architect and security-engineering are both in quality. anti-cheat-integrity (whose parent is security-engineering) sits in online with network-architect. docs/00 line 35 routes only two cases: disputes inside one lead's subtree go to that lead, and \"only cross-workstream disputes reach engine-architect\". Taken literally, that leaves a dispute between two leads in the same workstream \n6\t===== K-ARCH-4 ui-architect (21 caps UI.FW.*), content-pipeline-architect (20 caps incl. CNT.COOK.ddc/distributed/shared-cache/world-build/on-demand), physics-architect (18 caps incl. PHY.ARCH.rewind/streaming/async/events/fields/multi-world/local-frames/tier-transitions), platform-architect (27)\n7\tF: Several leads are also the main implementers of large subsystems, and they arbitrate disputes in their own subtree while being a party to them. ui-architect owns the whole retained UI runtime (layout, styling, animation, world UI, maps, loading screens, subtitle presentation) and there is no UI runtime expert. content-pipeline-architect owns DDC, distributed cook and the cook server. physics-architect owns rewind, streaming insertion, fields, multi-world and local frames.\n8\tP: Add expert 'ui-runtime-framework' owning UI.FW.layout/styling/animation/world-ui/performance/maps/loading-screens/subtitles/validation. Add tool expert 'cook-infrastructure' owning CNT.COOK.ddc/distributed/shared-cache/on-demand/world-build. Add expert 'physics-world-runtime' owning PHY.ARCH.rewind/streaming/async/events/fields/multi-world/local-frames/tier-transitions. Leads keep policy and contract ownership.\n9\t V fact True The data supports the finding. In data/capabilities.json, ui-architect owns 21 capabilities. These include the whole runtime surface: UI.FW.layout, styling, animation, world-ui, performance, maps, loading-screens, subtitles and validation. Its only child skills are text-fonts and localization-i18n, so no UI runtime expert exists. content-pipeline-architect owns 20, including CNT.COOK.ddc, distributed, shared-cache, world-build and on-demand. Its children are asset-import-interchange and asset-cook-processors, which handle format and generic processors, not DDC or cook-server infrastructure. physics-architect owns 18, including PHY.ARCH.rewind, async, streaming, events, multi-world, local-fra\n10\t V materiality True I checked data/capabilities.json and data/skills.json, and the facts hold. ui-architect owns 21 caps, content-pipeline-architect 20, physics-architect 18 and platform-architect 27. The largest expert owns 17 and the median expert owns 8. So these leads are the biggest capability owners in the framework, which runs against the tier definition in docs/00 §2: leads own architecture and contracts, experts own and implement focused territories.  The UI case is the strongest. ui-architect's only children are text-fonts and localization-i18n, so no expert owns the retained UI runtime. The lead implements layout, styling, animation, world UI, maps, loading screens, subtitles and validation. docs/00 \n11\t===== K-RENDER-5 C-RTAS, ray-tracing-infrastructure, RND.GRAPH.external-work, L28, spatial-audio-acoustics\n12\tF: RND.GRAPH.external-work declares acceleration-structure builds as producers that the render graph schedules, with barriers, queue placement and budgets. But C-RTAS (build/refit/compaction and ray queries) is layer 2, below C-RG (layer 3), and the C-RTAS module does not consume C-RG (only C-RG@C-RT does). The layering therefore forbids the AS-build module from registering with the graph, so it must record its own barriers and submissions. That reintroduces renderer sync outside the graph (L28), including for the non-render consumer spatial-audio-acoustics.\n13\tP: Move C-RTAS to layer 3 with requires [C-RG, C-GPUMEM], and make ray-tracing-infrastructure consume C-RG unattributed. Alternatively, add an L2 contract C-GPUWORK owned by render-graph-scheduling (external GPU work registration: producer, queue class, deadline, budget, resource import/export). C-RTAS, C-GPUMEM uploads and C-IO GPU decompression would require it, and the graph would implement it.\n14\t V fact False The finding is right about the layers, but the framework already covers the case, so the deficiency does not exist as described. What checks out in data/contracts.json and data/skills.json: C-RTAS is layer 2 and requires only [C-RHI, C-GPUMEM]. C-RG is layer 3. ray-tracing-infrastructure consumes C-RG only as C-RG@C-RT. spatial-audio-acoustics consumes C-RTAS?. RND.GRAPH.external-work lists \"AS builds\" as registered producers. What the finding misses: (1) RND.GRAPH.external-work also covers \"import of externally written resources, queue-ownership transfer & first-use acquire\". L28's default stance says exactly this: \"Render-graph-derived barriers; external work imported with queue transfers\"\n15\t V materiality False The finding's central inference does not hold. It claims that because C-RTAS (L2) does not require C-RG (L3), the AS-build module is barred from being scheduled by the graph and so has to issue its own barriers and submissions. The framework, however, consistently models external GPU work by inversion. Low-level producers expose work items, and the graph, at the higher layer, imports and schedules them. C-GPUMEM (L2) uploads and C-IO (L2) GPU decompression are listed as RND.GRAPH.external-work producers in exactly the same way, and neither requires C-RG. C-IO's summary spells the pattern out: work is \"declared as external work items executed as render-graph nodes\", and C-RG requires C-GPUMEM\n16\t===== K-RENDER-6 path-tracing, RND.PT.reference, QA.RENDER.reference, ANM.CINE.movie-render, render-validation, cinematics-sequencer\n17\tF: The reference path tracer is the ground-truth oracle for all raster features, yet it provides no contract. render-validation and cinematics movie render reach it through no declared interface (convergence target, seeds, AOVs, tolerance). The oracle also consumes C-LIGHT (the sampling API of direct-lighting-shadows, one of the skills it judges) and C-TEMPORAL (the denoiser/upscaler). A light-units or IES bug in C-LIGHT, or a denoiser bias, therefore appears identically in raster and reference, and the goldens pass. The oracle is not independent of what it grades. It also shares a lead and a workstream with the judged features.\n18\tP: Add C-PTREF (layer 3, owner path-tracing, requires C-RSCENE and C-MATIF, conformance, oracle_author render-validation): reference render request (scene snapshot, view, convergence criterion, seed, AOVs) and converged, undenoised output with a variance estimate. Attribute C-LIGHT and C-TEMPORAL to the real-time module only. render-validation and cinematics-sequencer consume C-PTREF?. Add an independence row: path-tracing vs direct-lighting-shadows/global-illumination, no shared sampling code.\n19\t V fact True The finding checks out against the data. In skills.json, path-tracing has provides: [] even though its purpose says \"Reference path tracer as ground-truth oracle for validation\". It consumes C-LIGHT and C-TEMPORAL (plus C-RT?, C-MATIF, C-RSCENE, C-INSTANCES, C-SCENETEX).  C-LIGHT is owned by direct-lighting-shadows, whose purpose covers light units and profiles. So a C-LIGHT bug would feed both the raster output and the reference it is compared against.  No contract in contracts.json covers a reference-render request or result under any name. The grep for reference, ground truth and undenoised found no such contract. render-validation's purpose names \"comparisons against the reference path t\n20\t V materiality True The data confirms the facts. In data/skills.json, path-tracing has provides=[] and consumes C-LIGHT and C-TEMPORAL, even though RND.PT.reference in data/capabilities.json calls it the \"ground-truth oracle\". QA.RENDER.reference (render-validation) and ANM.CINE.movie-render (cinematics-sequencer) both list path-tracing as a contributor, but neither skill consumes any contract from it. So the oracle every golden image is judged against has no declared request/output interface: no convergence criterion, seed, AOVs or variance. Autonomous agents coordinate through contracts, so this is a real missing contract.  The independence concern also holds. C-LIGHT covers light units and the many-light sam\n21\t===== K-SIM-11 untrusted-inputs.json, PHY.COL.runtime-build, WLD.VOX.edits, sandbox-online-server\n22\tF: Server-side runtime collision generation driven by remote edits or UGC meshes has no untrusted-input entry. Pathological geometry is a CPU denial-of-service with no validating owner or limits.\n23\tP: Add input 'runtime-collision-geometry': validating_owner collision-detection, parser_owners [voxel-worlds, modding-ugc], hostile-remote, harness, limits 'tri count/extents/degeneracy/rebuild time per tick'.\n24\t V fact False It is true that untrusted-inputs.json has no entry for collision geometry, meshes or voxel edits. But the finding's main claim, that runtime collision building is a CPU denial-of-service with \"no validating owner or limits\", is already covered under other wording. (1) PHY.COL.runtime-build (owner collision-detection, with voxel-worlds and modding-ugc as contributors) is defined as \"Runtime/incremental collision generation for edited geometry (voxel chunks, deformed heightfields, debris, UGC), async and budgeted\", so rebuild time is already budgeted. (2) NET.SRV.budgets (dedicated-server) is \"Per-connection memory/CPU/RPC quotas & limits on client-triggered work\", which bounds rebuilds trigge\n25\t V materiality False I don't think this finding holds as a material defect. The registry lists hostile data that crosses a trust boundary, and each entry has a parser. Collision geometry is not such an input. It is built on the server from data that already has an entry with limits. Remote voxel and construction edits arrive as input-commands or replicated-state (hostile-remote, with rate/tick-window and count/depth/rate limits). UGC meshes arrive as mods or assets (size/depth/count, validated by modding-ugc and serialization-schema). Voxel chunk meshes are well-formed by construction and bounded per chunk, so the real denial-of-service lever is how often and how many edits a player can make. NET.SRV.budgets cov\n26\t===== K-TOOLS-8 team-large profile; CNT.COOK.shared-cache, ED.COLLAB.multiuser, ED.ARCH.telemetry, CNT.COOK.distributed; configurations\n27\tF: Collaboration scales through one binary team-large add-on, used only by aaa-open-world-online-tools. The 10–150-person tier (std3d/online studios) is neither designed nor proven, and a shared DDC, the largest iteration win even for 3–5 people, is gated as AAA-only. The brief's 3→1000 ladder is not represented.\n28\tP: Remove team-large from CNT.COOK.shared-cache and keep a CNT.COOK.cache-fleet [team-large] for multi-site deployment. Add a team-mid add-on (shared DDC, tool telemetry, small-session multi-user, partial sync) and configuration standard-3d-team-tools {std3d, online, team-mid} claimed at M4. Tag ED.COLLAB.multiuser [team-mid, team-large].\n29\t V fact True I checked this against the data files and the finding holds. In data/capabilities.json, CNT.COOK.shared-cache (\"Team/cloud derived-data cache deployment & health\"), CNT.COOK.distributed, ED.ARCH.telemetry and ED.COLLAB.multiuser (\"Session-based multi-user editing (small sessions)\") are each tagged only [team-large]. In data/skills.json the add-ons list has team-large and nothing for mid-size teams: no team-mid or similar entry. Among the configurations, team-large appears only in aaa-open-world-online-{client,server,tools}. The other tools configurations (indie-2d-tools, standard-3d-tools, online-3d-tools, minimal-tools, lite-3d-mobile-tools) do not have it, so none of them gets a shared DDC\n30\t V materiality True The finding holds. The data matches what it says: CNT.COOK.shared-cache, ED.COLLAB.multiuser, ED.ARCH.telemetry and CNT.COOK.distributed are all tagged only `team-large` in data/capabilities.json. `team-large` appears only in the M6 exit, on aaa-open-world-online-tools. The standard-3d-tools, lite-3d-mobile-tools, online-3d-tools, indie-2d-tools and minimal-tools configurations have no shared DDC, no telemetry and no multi-user editing.  There is also an internal contradiction. ED.COLLAB.multiuser is titled \"Session-based multi-user editing (small sessions)\", yet only the AAA profile can use it. ED.ARCH.telemetry covers \"DDC/cook health\", which matters to any studio.  The gap is not covered \n31\t===== K-PLATFORM-1 platform-console, rhi-console, certification-compliance, C-CERT, platform-services, xr-runtime (PLAT.XR.runtime-backends), input-devices-haptics, network-transport, gpu-performance (PRF.GPU.tools), build-system-toolchains (BLD.SYS.platform-sdks), ci-cd-automation (BLD.CI.device-lanes), crash-diagnostics, accessibility, PLAT.CON.confidential-slots, ARCH.STRUCT.platform-backends\n32\tF: Confidentiality is modelled per skill: only platform-console and rhi-console carry an NDA class, and check.py forbids an NDA class on any skill not tagged console-only. Much NDA material sits in untagged skills with no access class: console TRC text in certification-compliance (published to every skill through the universal C-CERT), first-party service SDKs in platform-services (docs/00 §8 calls them 'NDA SDKs'), the console XR SDK in xr-runtime, console pad and adaptive-trigger libraries, console sockets and relays, console GPU profilers, console compilers and SDKs, devkit lanes, console crash dumps and console TTS. ARCH.STRUCT.platform-backends lists input, sockets and the IME/a11y bridge as slots, but PLAT.CON.confidential-slots omits all three. Result: either unlicensed agents get NDA documents, or these capabilities have no owner allowed to build them for consoles.\n33\tP: Add a capability-level / module-level access field (e.g. cap option 'access':'nda:<holder>' and implements 'C-X@nda:<holder>'). Allow per-holder confidential sub-instances inside non-console skills, and change the check rule to 'NDA-classed caps/modules only in console-tagged instances'. Split C-CERT into a public register (id, owner capability, sanitized paraphrase) and per-holder confidential partitions. Add input/haptics, sockets/relay and IME/TTS bridge to PLAT.CON.confidential-slots.\n34\t V fact True I checked each factual claim against the data files, and they hold.  1. **Only two skills carry an access class.** In skills.json, only platform-console and rhi-console have `access` (\"nda:per-platform-holder\"). The other targeted skills have neither platforms nor access set: certification-compliance, platform-services, xr-runtime, input-devices-haptics, network-transport, gpu-performance, build-system-toolchains, ci-cd-automation, crash-diagnostics and accessibility.  2. **check.py forbids NDA on any non-console skill.** Lines 236-239 reject an `nda*` access class on any skill whose platforms are not exactly [\"console\"]. The access model is per skill only. C-ORCH also says \"access classes p\n35\t V materiality False This finding does not hold as a material wrong-boundary blocker. The framework already made and recorded a reasoned decision about this boundary. docs/00 §8 says console skills carry an NDA access class and are instantiated per platform holder, citing PLAT.PAL.confidential-extensions. PLAT.CON.confidential-slots then puts every confidential console implementation of a registered slot in platform-console (nda:per-platform-holder), behind public interfaces that the domain skills own. So the domain skills (platform-services, xr-runtime, crash-diagnostics, input and others) own public interfaces and emulation, and the NDA code lives in the per-holder console instance. The framework does not need\n36\t===== K-PLATFORM-7 C-SVC, platform-services, platform-desktop, platform-mobile, platform-web, ARCH.REQ.platform-matrix, configurations\n37\tF: C-SVC is not needs_implementer, and only platform-console implements it. Store and service SDK backends have no platform-tagged owner and no closure proof: Steamworks, EOS/EGS, GOG Galaxy, Microsoft Store/GDK-on-PC, GameKit/StoreKit, Play Games/Play Billing, EU-DMA alternative marketplaces and web-portal SDKs. Storefront is also missing as a configuration axis, although the same pc build differs per store in entitlements/DRM, overlay, achievements, packaging and submission.\n38\tP: Make C-SVC needs_implementer with a null/DRM-free backend. Add storefronts to configurations. Add PLAT.DESK.storefronts (platform-desktop), PLAT.MOB.storefronts (platform-mobile) and PLAT.WEB.portals (platform-web), each implementing C-SVC for its platform. platform-services keeps only the cross-store model.\n39\t V fact False Some of the finding's structural statements are true. C-SVC is not needs_implementer: only C-PAL, C-RHI and C-TARGETPLAT are. Only platform-console lists C-SVC under implements. No configuration in skills.json has a storefront axis. The core claim, that store and service SDK backends have no owner and no closure proof, does not hold up against the data. platform-services has platforms=null, so it covers every platform, and profiles=[\"all\"], so it is in every configuration. It provides C-SVC and owns the store-backend capabilities PLAT.SVC.entitlements (\"Entitlements, store, DLC ownership\"), identity, achievements, leaderboards, cloud-save, trials and first-party-emulation. Its purpose covers\n40\t V materiality False The finding rests on \"no owner\", and that premise is weak. platform-services provides C-SVC and carries no platform tag, so it applies to every platform. Its purpose covers \"First-party platform and store services... entitlements and commerce through first-party stores\", and it lists \"platform SDKs\" as expertise. It also owns PLAT.SVC.entitlements (\"Entitlements, store, DLC ownership\") and PLAT.COMM.iap. platform-architect's non_responsibilities send \"Store and online services\" to platform-services. C-NETLINK already says Steam/EOS transports are backends implemented by platform-services and online-services-liveops.  platform-console implements C-SVC only because of the NDA access class. Ste\n41\t===== K-PLATFORM-8 rhi-d3d12 (platforms ['pc']), rhi-console, RND.RHI.console\n42\tF: rhi-d3d12 is PC-only, and every console graphics API goes to rhi-console as an independent backend. The Xbox Series API is a D3D12 superset that shares most backend code, DXC and the barrier model with PC D3D12. A second NDA agent would re-implement D3D12 and the two would drift. GDK runtime code shared by PC and console (GameInput, XGameRuntime) is split the same way.\n43\tP: Model the Xbox-class backend as a confidential module of rhi-d3d12 (implements 'C-RHI@nda:xbox', access per K-PLATFORM-1), with rhi-d3d12 as the base. Restrict rhi-console to non-D3D12 holder APIs. Apply the same pattern to GDK-shared PAL code between platform-desktop and platform-console.\n44\t V fact True The deficiency exists as described. In data/skills.json, rhi-d3d12 has platforms ['pc'] and its purpose covers only PC D3D12 (Agility SDK). rhi-console (platforms ['console'], access nda:per-platform-holder, one instance per holder) implements C-RHI on \"each console graphics API\" as an independent backend. RND.RHI.console, -validation and -quirks all sit in rhi-console. No capability, contract summary or skill purpose treats the Xbox backend as a shared D3D12 base with a confidential extension. Searches for superset, shared backend, base backend and similar wording found nothing. PLAT.PAL.confidential-extensions (in platform-architect) is only a generic mechanism for segregated modules and p\n45\t V materiality True The finding is material and within the critic's mandate, and the proposal is technically sound, though it depends on K-PLATFORM-1.  Facts check out in data/skills.json. rhi-d3d12 has platforms [\"pc\"], no access class, and implements C-RHI. rhi-console has platforms [\"console\"], access nda:per-platform-holder, and implements C-RHI \"on each console graphics API\". platform-desktop (Win32/GameInput, pc) and platform-console (NDA, console) both implement C-PAL on their own. scripts/check.py lines 236-239 forbid an NDA class on any skill not tagged console-only. So the framework cannot express a confidential extension of a PC skill, and the only way to model the Xbox-class D3D12 backend is a secon\n46\t===== K-PLATFORM-12 platform-desktop (macOS), platform-mobile (iOS/iPadOS/visionOS), rhi-metal\n47\tF: Apple support is split by form factor into two experts, so shared frameworks get implemented twice: GameController, GameKit, StoreKit, CAMetalLayer surfaces, AVAudioSession/CoreAudio, signing/notarization and TCC. tvOS is absent altogether.\n48\tP: Add a shared apple-common module attribution owned by platform-mobile, which platform-desktop consumes for macOS. Record tvOS as supported or as a non-goal in ARCH.REQ.platform-matrix.\n49\t V fact False The central claim, that the Apple frameworks would be implemented twice, is wrong: in data/capabilities.json each named area already has one cross-platform owner rather than platform-desktop and platform-mobile. Owners: GameKit (achievements) is PLAT.SVC.achievements and StoreKit (store and entitlements) is PLAT.SVC.entitlements, both owned by platform-services. GameController is INP.DEV.gamepads (input-devices-haptics). CoreAudio and AVAudioSession are AUD.ARCH.devices (audio-architect). CAMetalLayer surfaces are PLAT.PAL.windowing (platform-architect) with RND.RHI.present (rhi-core) and RND.RHI.metal (rhi-metal). Signing and notarization are BLD.REL.packaging (packaging-release-patching). \n50\t V materiality False The main claim, that shared Apple frameworks get implemented twice, does not survive a check of data/skills.json and docs/01. Most of the named frameworks are already owned once by skills that span platforms, not by the form-factor experts. GameKit and StoreKit (achievements, commerce, entitlements) belong to platform-services, whose platforms field is unrestricted. GameController (gamepads, hotplug, haptics) belongs to input-devices-haptics, also unrestricted. CoreAudio and AVAudioSession device backends belong to audio-architect. Packaging and signing belong to packaging-release-patching. The swapchain and surface (CAMetalLayer) are listed as a non-responsibility of platform-desktop and go\n51\t===== K-PROD-8 QA.FUNC area, OBS.CRASH, ED.COLLAB.production-tracking, ARCH.ORG.triage\n52\tF: Nothing owns bug capture: an in-game and in-editor bug report that bundles build ID, a replay/snapshot, screenshot or video, logs, position and config, deduplicates against crash buckets and files into the tracker. Testers, playtesters, beta players and creators file most defects this way. ARCH.PROD.intake covers only game-team reports to the engine.\n53\tP: Add QA.FUNC.bug-capture (functional-automation-soak) with contributors determinism-replay, crash-diagnostics, visual-debugging-tools and collaboration-version-control. Register a 'bug-reports' untrusted input for player and beta submissions.\n54\t V fact False The finding says nothing owns bug capture, but data/capabilities.json already has a capability with the exact id it proposes. QA.FUNC.bug-capture, \"In-game bug reporting with repro capture & tracker integration\", is owned by functional-automation-soak and lists crash-diagnostics, observability-telemetry and determinism-replay as contributors. OBS.CRASH.feedback, \"In-game bug/feedback capture boundary for players\", is also there, owned by crash-diagnostics. So the gap as described does not exist. The only parts not covered are small refinements: capture from inside the editor, extra contributors (visual-debugging-tools, collaboration-version-control), and a 'bug-reports' untrusted input. None\n55\t V materiality False The main claim is wrong, and the proposed change mostly repeats what the framework already has. data/capabilities.json already defines QA.FUNC.bug-capture (\"In-game bug reporting with repro capture & tracker integration\"). It is owned by functional-automation-soak, the same owner the finding proposes, with crash-diagnostics, observability-telemetry and determinism-replay as contributors. docs/01 (line 1664) and docs/03 (the owner and contributor lists) reflect the same entry, so \"Nothing owns bug capture\" is false. The only new parts would be two extra contributors (visual-debugging-tools, collaboration-version-control) and a separate 'bug-reports' entry in the untrusted-inputs list. The mai\n56\t===== K-FUTURE-1 C-RT, C-RTAS, C-MLGPU (gated), C-GPUTIER, RND.GI.hybrid, RND.PT.realtime, configurations\n57\tF: Gating is absolute: check.py only lets a skill consume C-RT, C-RTAS or C-MLGPU optionally, in every configuration. No tier or configuration can require hardware RT or GPU inference, so every std3d/aaa lighting and reconstruction feature must keep and validate a non-RT, non-ML path forever. RT-only renderers and an RT+ML console baseline cannot be expressed.\n58\tP: Add GPU tiers 'rt' and 'rt+tensor' to C-GPUTIER and a profile add-on 'hwrt'. Change the gate rule so modules whose capabilities carry that profile may consume C-RT/C-RTAS/C-MLGPU non-optionally. Add a configuration rt-required-3d-client (std3d+aaa+hwrt), claimed at M6. Update docs/00 §7: gated means optional below the declaring tier.\n59\t V fact True The finding is accurate. In scripts/check.py (lines 412-426), a skill that consumes a contract with gated=true must consume it optionally ('C-X?') unless it owns it. The rule applies in every configuration and has no tier or profile exemption. The selftest case 'gated contract required' (global-illumination adding C-RT) confirms this. In data/contracts.json, C-RT, C-RTAS, C-MLGPU, C-ML and C-AIAGENT are marked gated=true, and docs/00 section 7 (line 108) says they 'may only be consumed optionally'. The skills match: global-illumination consumes C-RT? and C-MLGPU?, path-tracing consumes C-RT?, and reconstruction-upscaling consumes C-MLGPU?. Nothing else already covers this. The profile add-on\n60\t V materiality True The facts check out. scripts/check.py (around lines 412-426) rejects any non-optional consumption of a gated contract, with no exception for any tier or profile. C-RT, C-RTAS and C-MLGPU are all flagged gated in data/contracts.json, and global-illumination and path-tracing consume them only as C-RT? and C-MLGPU?.  This is material, and it is a real boundary defect rather than a reasoned scope decision. docs/00 §7 justifies gating only as the route to \"emerging and experimental techniques\". Yet the core RT capabilities (RND.RT.as, instances, pipelines, software, lod) and RND.GI.hybrid (\"software + hardware RT\") are all established, and basic HW RT has no radar entry. C-RTAS's own summary says\n61\t===== K-FUTURE-15 WLD.PCG.ml\n62\tF: Tool-side ML generation (cooked and cached, so deterministic by construction; M) is conflated with runtime generation at stream-in (X). Putting all of it in the experimental profile blocks tool-side use that already ships.\n63\tP: Split into WLD.PCG.ml-bake (M, tool-side via C-COOK, provenance via ED.AI.provenance) and WLD.PCG.ml-runtime (X, experimental).\n64\t V fact False The finding is factually right about one thing. WLD.PCG.ml (\"ML-generated content\", owned by procedural-generation) is class X, carries only the \"experimental\" profile, and radar.json lists it as X with the fallback \"Rule-based PCG\". But the data does not show the gap the finding claims: tool-side ML generation already has mature (M) coverage under other ids.  - ED.AI.generative, \"Generative asset tools & editor copilots\", is class M. - ED.AI.provenance, \"Provenance of generated content (consumer of CNT.ID.rights)\", is class M. - ED.AI.evaluation, \"Evaluation harness for generative & ML-assisted tools\", is class M. - CNT.COOK.ml-assisted, \"ML-assisted retopology/UV/LOD techniques as processo\n65\t V materiality False The claimed harm does not hold. WLD.PCG.ml (\"ML-generated content\", X, experimental only) is one line, but the framework already covers tool-side ML generation at M. ED.AI.generative (\"Generative asset tools & editor copilots\", M) and ED.AI.provenance (M), both owned by ai-assisted-authoring in the aaa profile, cover exactly the editor tools cited as evidence: Unity AI and Roblox Cube 3D (ML terrain, texture and layout generation). So putting WLD.PCG.ml in the experimental profile does not block tool-side ML use that already ships. The radar entry's revisit trigger (\"Deterministic, licensable models with provenance\") and its evidence (\"Research & tool demos\") show that WLD.PCG.ml is aimed at\n66\t===== K-COMPLETE-6 NET.SESS, XC.EXT (modding-ugc), NET.SRV; configs indie-2d-online-moddable-*, sandbox-online-server\n67\tF: Community/player-hosted dedicated servers have no capability for redistributing the server binary, config/ruleset files, internet server listing and query protocol, or join-time content/mod manifest negotiation and download. NET.SESS.handshake negotiates versions and capabilities, not content. NET.TRANS.local-network is LAN only, and BLD.REL.end-of-service releases servers only at sunset. Sweep 3 (survival/sandbox).\n68\tP: Add NET.SESS.content-sync 'Join-time content/mod manifest negotiation, download & C-SIGN verification before load' (owner net-session, contributor modding-ugc, E). Add NET.SRV.community-hosting 'Player-hosted dedicated servers: redistributable build, config/ruleset files, listing registration & query protocol' (owner dedicated-server, contributors packaging-release-patching, online-services-liveops, E). Register both in untrusted-inputs.json.\n69\t V fact True I checked this against data/capabilities.json and the gap is real. NET.SESS (handshake, join, baseline, reconnect, travel, disconnect, host-mode, auth) has nothing for content or mod manifests. NET.SESS.handshake only covers the version check, auth token and capability negotiation. NET.SRV (headless, density, orchestration, observability, persistence, transactions, zoning, lifecycle, admin, overload, budgets, cross-server) has no player-hosted redistribution, config/ruleset files, or listing/query protocol. XC.EXT covers mod-loading, mod-compat, ugc-discovery and ugc-integrity (C-SIGN distribution signing), but none of them syncs a server's mod set to a joining client. BLD.REL.end-of-service\n70\t V materiality True This is a real, material gap. I confirmed it in data/capabilities.json. NET.SESS.handshake covers version/auth/capability negotiation only. XC.EXT.mod-loading, mod-compat, ugc and ugc-integrity cover local loading, patch compatibility and the UGC/Workshop pipeline, but nothing covers a server pushing its content set when a client joins. NET.TRANS.local-network is LAN only. BLD.REL.server-artifacts is container images for fleets. BLD.REL.end-of-service releases community servers only at sunset. No capability owns the config/ruleset files for a redistributable player-hosted server or the internet query/listing protocol. The indie-2d-online-moddable-client/server pair (M4) and sandbox-online-se\n71\t===== K-COMPLETE-12 ED.UI / ED.WORLD\n72\tF: There is no scene outliner/hierarchy panel (virtualized list over millions of entities, folders, filtering, partition/layer awareness). ED.WORLD.ld-utilities covers filters and layer visibility only. Sweep 1.\n73\tP: Add ED.UI.outliner 'Scene outliner: virtualized hierarchy/list over world & partition data, filtering, folders' (owner editor-ui-framework, contributor world-editor-viewport, E).\n74\t V fact True The claim is accurate. I searched every data/*.json file for outliner, hierarchy, scene tree/dock, entity/actor/world browser and tree view. Nothing names a scene outliner or hierarchy panel. The only \"hierarchy\" hits are runtime transform hierarchies, cluster LOD and hierarchical pathfinding. None of these is an editor navigation panel. The ED.UI capabilities are toolkit, inspectors, asset-browser, ux, asset-editor-host, thumbnails, curves-timeline, tabular, toolkit-basis and localization. The ED.WORLD capabilities are viewport, gizmos, placement, partitioned, splines, modes, preview-scenes, blockout, ld-utilities (\"measure, filters, bulk replace, layer visibility\"), 2d-mode, color-manageme\n75\t V materiality True I confirmed the gap in data/capabilities.json. ED.UI has toolkit, inspectors, asset-browser, ux, asset-editor-host, thumbnails, curves-timeline, tabular, toolkit-basis and localization. ED.WORLD.ld-utilities covers measure, filters, bulk replace and layer visibility. ED.ARCH.selection covers the selection model and ED.ARCH.scale covers editor performance in general. None of them owns the scene hierarchy or outliner view. docs/06 section C and docs/00 record no decision to leave it out. The L07 principle (hierarchy only where attachment needs it) is about runtime data, not this editor panel. The outliner is a real omission because the map already lists panels at this level of detail (asset br\n76\t===== K-GAMEPLAY-4 gameplay-camera, C-VIEW, gameplay-systems-toolkit, character-movement, narrative-dialogue, GAM.CAM.rigs, GAM.CAM.comfort\n77\tF: gameplay-camera provides no contract. Abilities (aim/zoom), movement (mount/vehicle/swim), dialogue framing, vehicles and game code have no API to request camera modes, hints or shakes, so their only option is to write their own C-VIEW source and skip rig, collision and blending. C-VIEW also puts shake/FOV channels in spatial-transforms, while shake content and its accessibility scaling are gameplay-camera territory.\n78\tP: Add C-CAMERA (layer 4, owner gameplay-camera): 'camera mode/hint requests with priority and blend, per-local-player rig binding, shake/impulse assets scaled by a11y settings, framing targets'. Add C-CAMERA? to consumes of gameplay-systems-toolkit, character-movement, narrative-dialogue, vehicle-physics and cinematics-sequencer. Restrict C-VIEW's shake/FOV channels to final composition of view sources.\n79\t V fact True The finding holds against the data files. In data/skills.json, gameplay-camera has provides=[] and consumes C-VIEW, C-GAME, C-PHYS?, C-INPUT? and C-A11YRT?. data/contracts.json has no camera request, hint, impulse or framing contract: a search for those terms and \"camera mode\" finds nothing. The only related contract is C-VIEW (layer 2, owner spatial-transforms). Its summary covers view sources \"with priority/blend stack and shake/FOV channels\". So any caller that wants a camera effect has to register its own C-VIEW source or write to those shake/FOV channels directly. That path skips gameplay-camera's rigs, collision and blending (GAM.CAM.rigs), and nothing in the data requires shake from o\n80\t V materiality False The defect is real and in scope for K-GAMEPLAY. gameplay-camera has provides=[] (data/skills.json:7850ff). Abilities, movement, dialogue and cinematics can only reach the camera by consuming C-VIEW and pushing their own view sources, which skips rig collision and blending. C-VIEW (L2, owned by spatial-transforms) holds the shake and FOV channels. spatial-transforms does not consume C-A11YRT, but GAM.CAM.comfort (shake, FOV, head-bob) belongs to gameplay-camera. So comfort scaling cannot reach every caller's shake. Neither docs/06 section C nor docs/00 records a scope decision against a camera request contract. docs/00:118 only puts arbitration in C-VIEW. For autonomous agents this missing se\n81\t===== K-GAMEPLAY-7 UI.LOC.messageformat, C-LOC, UI.LOC.gather, gameplay-data, radar.json\n82\tF: Only message-level plurals and gender are covered. Nothing covers grammatical agreement with substituted terms: item, character and place names inserted into sentences need per-locale gender, animacy, case forms, articles, elision and particles. gameplay-data rows need these attributes, or 'You picked up {item}' is ungrammatical in Slavic, Germanic, Romance, Semitic and Korean text.\n83\tP: Add UI.LOC.terms 'Localizable terms with per-locale grammatical attributes (gender, animacy, case forms, articles, particles) and agreement in substituted messages' (owner localization-i18n; contributors gameplay-data, narrative-dialogue; E). Add a radar entry for MessageFormat 2.0 adoption (M, fallback ICU MF1 + term attributes). Extend the C-LOC summary with term references.\n84\t V fact True The finding is accurate. Searching every data/*.json file found nothing on per-locale grammatical attributes for substituted terms. There is no mention of agreement with an inserted term, case or declension forms, animacy, articles, elision, particles or postpositions, and no match for Fluent or MessageFormat 2.0. The relevant data says only this: UI.LOC.messageformat is 'Plurals & gender (ICU MessageFormat class)'. UI.LOC.generated covers plural/gender agreement for runtime-generated text only. UI.LOC.gather is only a localizable-text value type plus gather. The C-LOC summary is 'String IDs, formatting, locale switching.' The localization-i18n purpose also names only ICU-class formatting. N\n85\t V materiality True I confirmed this against the data. UI.LOC holds only \"Plurals & gender (ICU MessageFormat class)\" and UI.LOC.generated, which covers plural and gender agreement for runtime-generated text only. UI.LOC.gather is just a localizable-text value type plus a gather step. C-LOC's summary reads \"String IDs, formatting, locale switching.\" Nothing gives substituted terms (item, character or place names from gameplay-data or narrative-dialogue) per-locale grammatical attributes. That means gender, animacy, case forms, articles and particles, and no path carries those attributes through reflection or gather into message agreement. ICU MF1 gender select only works if the caller already has the attribute,\n86\t===== K-GAMEPLAY-14 AUD.SPAT.propagation, radar 'Wave-based acoustic propagation'\n87\tF: Precomputed wave-based and ray-traced acoustic propagation is labeled M despite years of shipping in AAA.\n88\tP: Relabel AUD.SPAT.propagation as E. If a frontier is needed, add AUD.SPAT.propagation-dynamic (M, real-time GPU/RT propagation for dynamic geometry) with fallback AUD.SPAT.propagation.\n89\t V fact False The data matches what the finding says. AUD.SPAT.propagation is \"Acoustic propagation (ray-traced, precomputed wave)\", owned by spatial-audio-acoustics and marked M in data/capabilities.json. The radar entry \"Wave-based acoustic propagation\" in data/radar.json is class M, cites \"Project Acoustics; Steam Audio\" as evidence and gives \"Reverb zones + occlusion\" as the fallback. No AUD.SPAT.propagation-dynamic or any other split exists. check.py only checks that radar class and capability maturity agree, so it does not prove the label is right. The problem is the conclusion that the label is an error. The framework defines emerging in docs/01-capability-map.md as \"shipping in some titles, evalua\n90\t V materiality False The finding misreads the label. In data/capabilities.json, M is defined as \"emerging production\", and docs/01 line 5 spells it out as \"shipping in some titles, evaluate per ADR\". So the M label already says the technique ships. It does not say the technique is unproven. The critic's evidence (Triton/Project Acoustics in Gears 4/5 and Sea of Thieves, Steam Audio) supports M: the technique ships in a limited set of titles and has not become the industry default. Most shipping titles still rely on the radar's recorded fallback, \"Reverb zones + occlusion\", and that fallback is itself labeled E (AUD.SPAT.environments, AUD.SPAT.occlusion). The revisit trigger \"Bake/runtime cost fit\" is a sensible \n91\t===== K-TEST-4 oracle_author of C-PAL (accessibility), C-SIGN (modding-ugc), C-ML (anti-cheat-integrity), C-MLGPU (texture-streaming-vt), C-EDHOST (fluid-simulation), C-VT (render-2d-vector), C-SYNC (entity-object-model), C-RTAS (spatial-audio-acoustics)\n92\tF: Oracle authors look assigned by rotating through consumers, not by who can build the oracle. Crypto needs known-answer and edge-case vectors, lock-free code needs linearizability and model checking, and ML inference needs parity against a reference framework. A single arbitrary consumer cannot author these. No contract names the external reference corpus its oracle must include.\n93\tP: Add an oracle_reference field (list of external corpora) to every code contract, required by check.py for layers 0–2. Allow a quality skill to be oracle_author, co-authoring with a consumer: C-SIGN→security-engineering; C-SYNC and C-TASK→robustness-fuzzing with a consumer; C-ML and C-MLGPU→render-validation or simulation-validation with a consumer; C-PAL→platform-desktop consumer plus test-runtime-harness.\n94\t V fact True I checked this against data/contracts.json and skills.json. All 8 oracle_author assignments are exactly as claimed: C-PAL→accessibility, C-SIGN→modding-ugc, C-ML→anti-cheat-integrity, C-MLGPU→texture-streaming-vt, C-EDHOST→fluid-simulation, C-VT→render-2d-vector, C-SYNC→entity-object-model, C-RTAS→spatial-audio-acoustics. C-TASK→animation-architect also fits the pattern. These authors lack the relevant expertise, and 4 of the 8 (anti-cheat-integrity, texture-streaming-vt, fluid-simulation, spatial-audio-acoustics) do not even list the contract in 'consumes'. No contract has an oracle_reference field or anything like it. No data file, doc or capability mentions Wycheproof, CAVP, crypto known-\n95\t V materiality True The finding holds and \"major\" is the right severity. The pattern it describes is real in data/contracts.json. Oracle authors are spread thinly across unrelated skills: C-EDHOST goes to fluid-simulation, C-RTAS to spatial-audio-acoustics, C-ML to anti-cheat-integrity, C-MLGPU to texture-streaming-vt, C-SYNC to entity-object-model, C-TASK to animation-architect and C-PAL to accessibility. By my approximate `consumes` check, many authors (such as fluid-simulation for C-EDHOST, and C-ML, C-MLGPU and C-RTAS) do not even consume the contract. check.py only checks that the oracle_author exists and is not the sole implementer (lines 523-532). No contract has a field for reference corpora.  This is m\n96\t===== K-TEST-6 QA.AGENT.holdout; skills.json access classes; ARCH.ORG.write-sets\n97\tF: The framework says acceptance suites are 'read-only for implementers' with 'sealed holdouts', but nothing enforces it. The only access class is NDA, and write-sets control writes, not reads. An implementer agent can read holdouts and expected outputs and fit its code to them.\n98\tP: Add access class sealed:oracle (holdouts, gate-time seeds, expected outputs), readable only by oracle authors and CI runners. Add BLD.CI.sealed-suites, owned by ci-cd-automation with contributor test-architect: execution only inside CI, results reported as pass/fail plus failure category. check.py ensures no implementer holds sealed:oracle access for its own contract.\n99\t V fact True The finding holds on the data. QA.AGENT.holdout (capabilities.json ~L10602) only says \"Sealed holdout partitions ... plus gate-time seeded property & metamorphic cases\". It is owned by test-architect with program-orchestration as contributor, and nothing defines who can read them or where they run. C-ORCH (contracts.json L760) gives access classes per skill only as \"public | nda:<holder>\". Its only test-related restriction is \"tests/acceptance/<contract-id>/ ... read-only (oracle-ro) for implementers\", which limits writes, not reads. In skills.json, \"access\" is used only as nda:per-platform-holder. Several capabilities come close but none cover reads: QA.AGENT.oracle-independence (implemente\n100\t V materiality True I checked the finding against the data and it holds. The C-ORCH summary in data/contracts.json allows only two access classes per skill, \"public | nda:<holder>\". It also makes tests/acceptance/<contract-id>/ \"read-only (oracle-ro) for implementers\", which means implementers can read them; only writing is blocked. The single access value anywhere in skills.json is nda:per-platform-holder. QA.AGENT.holdout (\"Sealed holdout partitions ... gate-time seeded ... cases\", test-architect, E) is described in prose only. ARCH.ORG.write-sets governs writes, and no check.py docstring item covers who can read a holdout. XC.SEC.agent-boundary is also prose only (M).  So docs/00's rule that acceptance suite\n101\t===== K-SEC-6 skills.json access classes; XC.SEC.agent-boundary; ARCH.ORG.human-gates\n102\tF: The only access class in the data is NDA. Nothing records per skill which agents have network egress, which read production player data (crash dumps, support tools, telemetry), which may write to CI or sensitive paths, or which may use secrets. Least privilege therefore cannot reach SKILL.md, and nothing prevents a role from combining untrusted input, private data and egress.\n103\tP: Add a per-skill 'access' object with fields egress, data, ci and secrets. Add a check.py rule that no skill with a redteam input or web egress may also hold player-PII/production data or CI write. Add 'agent access to production player data' to ARCH.ORG.human-gates.\n104\t===== K-SEC-8 untrusted-inputs project-content-to-llm (ai-assisted-authoring, M6); ED.ARCH.llm-agent-frontend, ED.ARCH.automation-security (editor-architect, M2)\n105\tF: The editor LLM/agent front end, which can execute editor commands, exists from M2 and ingests project content. That input's validating owner arrives only at M6. The permission model belongs to editor-architect.\n106\tP: Set the validating owner to editor-architect, with ai-assisted-authoring as a parser owner. Add a check.py rule that the validating owner is staffed no later than the input's consumers.\n107\t===== K-SEC-12 untrusted-inputs editor-plugins (limit 'permissions'), mods; XC.EXT.plugin-trust; ED.ARCH.extension; ED.ARCH.process\n108\tF: Native editor plugins and Python tool scripts are modelled as a fuzzable input limited by a permission manifest. In-process native code ignores manifests; the real boundaries are the publisher signature, workspace trust, and out-of-process or WASM isolation.\n109\tP: Split the input into editor-plugins-native (signed publisher + workspace trust + human gate for unsigned) and editor-plugins-sandboxed (enforced by an isolated host). Add ED.ARCH.plugin-isolation (owner editor-architect). Reword XC.EXT.plugin-trust to say manifests are enforceable only in isolated hosts.\n110\t===== K-SEC-15 XC.SEC.tamper\n111\tF: The anti-tamper/DRM integration boundary is code but is owned by a process skill. The analogous XC.SEC.anticheat sits in a runtime skill.\n112\tP: Split into XC.SEC.tamper-policy (owner security-engineering) and XC.SEC.tamper-integration (owner packaging-release-patching, contributors anti-cheat-integrity and cpu-performance).\n113\t"}]},"uuid":"b96f6a93-47f6-4b48-9993-d3d19d0405bf","timestamp":"2026-09-30T07:43:14.734Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"10f72c79-0142-4235-86eb-9eee91e98622","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1","slug":"warm-pondering-kernighan"}
